@thesamparr I vibe coded my own blog platform with Claude Code

The frontpage banner inspired by yours

https://t.co/GpyOOnMVpk

@kimmonismus That actually is pretty smart to do. 

Selling enterprise software takes a long time: it requires a lot of meetings, a lot of convincing, and all that. If you get workers to use it at home on their own things, they're going to bring it to work and go, "Hey, this is really cool to use. Maybe we could use it for customer service, running our factory floor, or whatever." 

So it'll help enterprise adoption to have a consumer product people can use at home to run their own life and their own ship, while keeping everybody from going to Meta Muse or Grokbot.

The innovation extends across Europe’s industrial ecosystem. 

@autodesk, @AVEVAGroup, @Cadence, Falkor, @MercedesBenz and @Synopsys are also putting our technology to work, from faster design simulations to vehicle inspection and AI factory digital twins.

Read their stories in the blog above. 👆

@valeriixai My agent running at https://t.co/OSeoAG4VSQ was trained by me over months.

Really, it is how you train it and the rules you have it write.

But I want to catch mostly the big stuff. The small stuff I can see on X Pro (and it reads what I do to make its choices).

@alexander__main @bot Use the right tool for the job for sure. If it doesn't work for you, what does? And why? 

And it is getting better. So something that doesn't work today might work in a month. 

If you are a heavy user of X, it sure should know you better than others do.

@BrandGrowthOS Yes it is a mess of systems and cultural resistance.

And yes regulations that make it hard to build systems that interact with customers before and after visits.

Things are changing. Will be tracking them

@RaulJuncoV Yeah, there's definitely some of it going on.

People are clinging to a skill that was unique and valuable, but now it's worth very little.

For some, this was the only skill they had. For others, this was just one of their skills.

@TonyIsHere4You Right, but is that 100% of what you do every day? 

I also find that current models make mistakes when they touch certain parts of the code, but those are rare (and improving).

Most of what people do can already be automated.

@kylegallatin @chrisalbon The legends!  @BEBischof reminded me of it, I took a look again  was impressed by how great the second edition is (I loved the first edition already). 

Ya'll need to write a third edition reminding people of how relevant all this stuff is still to AI.  

Link to the book for anyone reading https://t.co/et7g3FBDib

